WEBVTT

00:00.675 --> 00:02.061
- [Voiceover] We've already
seen and you're probably

00:02.061 --> 00:04.527
getting tired of me
pointing it out repeatedly,

00:04.527 --> 00:07.445
that this yellow area right
over here, this area under

00:07.445 --> 00:09.550
the curve y is equal
to f of x and above the

00:09.550 --> 00:11.737
positive x-axis or I
guess I can say just above

00:11.737 --> 00:16.737
the x-axis between x
equals a and x equals b,

00:18.302 --> 00:20.470
that we can denote this area
right over here as the definite

00:20.470 --> 00:23.568
integral of from a to b of f of x dx.

00:24.122 --> 00:27.184
Now what I want to explore
in this video and it'll

00:27.184 --> 00:30.084
come up with kind of an
answer that you probably

00:30.084 --> 00:31.550
could have guessed on
your own, but at least get

00:31.550 --> 00:33.695
an intuition for it,
is that I want to start

00:33.695 --> 00:35.950
thinking about the area under the curve

00:35.950 --> 00:37.961
that's a scaled version of f of x.

00:38.141 --> 00:40.902
Let's say it's y is
equal to c times f of x.

00:41.354 --> 00:45.266
Y is equal to some number times

00:45.266 --> 00:47.116
f of x, so it's scaling f of x.

00:47.446 --> 00:49.836
And so I want this to be
kind of some arbitrary

00:49.836 --> 00:52.038
number, but just to help me
visualize, you know I have

00:52.038 --> 00:53.882
to draw something so I'm
just gonna kind of in my

00:53.882 --> 00:55.739
head let's just pretend the c is

00:55.739 --> 00:57.653
a three for visualization purposes.

00:58.055 --> 01:00.190
So it's going to be three
times, so instead of one,

01:00.190 --> 01:01.488
instead of this far right over here

01:01.488 --> 01:02.964
it's going to be about this far.

01:03.130 --> 01:05.381
For right over here, instead
of this far right over

01:05.381 --> 01:09.114
here it's going to be that
and another right over there.

01:09.502 --> 01:13.495
And then instead of it's
going to be about there.

01:13.702 --> 01:16.917
And then instead of it
being like that it's going

01:16.917 --> 01:21.078
to be one, two and then
three, right around there.

01:21.537 --> 01:23.690
So I'm starting to get a
sense of what this curve

01:23.690 --> 01:28.568
is going to look like, a
scaled version of f of x.

01:28.697 --> 01:30.966
And at least what I'm
drawing is pretty close to

01:30.966 --> 01:34.250
three times f of x, but
just to give you an idea

01:34.250 --> 01:35.959
is going to look something like,

01:35.959 --> 01:37.741
and let's see over here if this distance,

01:37.741 --> 01:39.486
do a second one, a third
one, is gonna be up here.

01:39.493 --> 01:42.511
It's gonna look something like this.

01:43.111 --> 01:44.915
It's gonna look something like that.

01:45.282 --> 01:47.916
So this is a scaled version
and the scale I did right here

01:47.916 --> 01:52.916
I assumed a positive c greater than zero,

01:53.660 --> 01:55.679
but this is just for
visualization purposes.

01:56.326 --> 01:58.811
Now what do we think the area under this

01:58.811 --> 02:01.570
curve is going to be between a and b?

02:01.877 --> 02:06.877
So what do we think this area

02:10.126 --> 02:11.766
right over here is going to be?

02:12.432 --> 02:14.390
Now we already know how we can denote it.

02:14.598 --> 02:17.602
That area right over there
is equal to the definite

02:17.602 --> 02:21.139
integral from a to b of the function

02:21.139 --> 02:25.312
we're integrating is c f of x dx.

02:26.035 --> 02:29.045
I guess to make the question
a little bit clearer,

02:29.045 --> 02:31.278
how does this relate to this?

02:31.352 --> 02:34.921
How does this green area
relate to this yellow area?

02:35.836 --> 02:38.025
Well one way to think
about it is we just scaled

02:38.025 --> 02:42.294
the vertical dimension up by
c, so one way that you could

02:42.294 --> 02:45.574
reason it is if I'm finding
the area of something,

02:46.190 --> 02:49.803
if I have the area of a
rectangle and I have the

02:49.803 --> 02:54.573
vertical dimension is
let's say I don't want to

02:54.573 --> 02:56.476
use those same letters
over and over again.

02:56.520 --> 02:59.359
Well let's say the
vertical dimension is alpha

02:59.359 --> 03:01.889
and the horizontal dimension is beta.

03:01.889 --> 03:06.643
We know that the area is
going to be alpha times beta.

03:07.047 --> 03:10.776
Now if I scale up the
vertical dimension by c,

03:10.776 --> 03:15.776
so instead of alpha this
is c times alpha and

03:16.339 --> 03:20.538
this is, the width is beta,
if I scale up the vertical

03:20.538 --> 03:25.520
dimension by c so this
is now c times alpha,

03:25.520 --> 03:26.640
what's the area going to be?

03:26.956 --> 03:31.094
Well it's going to be c alpha times beta,

03:31.094 --> 03:32.591
or another way to think of it,

03:34.374 --> 03:36.767
when I scale one of the
dimensions by c I take my

03:36.767 --> 03:40.127
old area and I scale
up my old area up by c.

03:40.423 --> 03:41.449
And that's what we're doing, we're scaling

03:41.449 --> 03:43.241
up the vertical dimension by c.

03:43.241 --> 03:44.847
When you multiply c times f of x,

03:44.847 --> 03:46.723
f of x is giving us the vertical height.

03:46.745 --> 03:48.937
Now obviously that
changes as our x changes,

03:48.937 --> 03:50.829
but when you think back
to the Reimann sums

03:50.829 --> 03:53.469
the f of x was what gave us
the height of our rectangles.

03:53.765 --> 03:56.730
We're now scaling up the
height or scaling I should say

03:56.730 --> 03:59.190
because we might be scaling
down depending on the c.

03:59.393 --> 04:02.408
We're scaling it, we're
scaling one dimension by c.

04:02.408 --> 04:03.982
If you scale one dimension by c

04:03.982 --> 04:05.681
you're gonna scale the area by c.

04:06.086 --> 04:08.973
So this right over here, the integral,

04:08.973 --> 04:10.081
let me just rewrite it.

04:10.321 --> 04:15.255
The integral from a to b of c f of x dx,

04:15.255 --> 04:17.407
that's just going to be
the scaled, we're just

04:17.407 --> 04:20.707
going to take the area of f of x,

04:20.707 --> 04:23.071
so let me do that in the same color.

04:23.963 --> 04:25.320
We're going to take the
area under the curve

04:25.320 --> 04:29.462
f of x from a to b f of x dx and

04:29.462 --> 04:34.462
we're just going to scale it up by this c.

04:37.207 --> 04:39.164
So you might say, "Okay
maybe I could have felt

04:39.164 --> 04:41.571
"that was, you know, if I
have a c inside the integral

04:41.571 --> 04:43.206
"now I can take the c
out of the integral",

04:43.206 --> 04:46.513
and once again this is
not a rigorous proof based

04:46.513 --> 04:49.044
on the definition of
the definite integral,

04:49.044 --> 04:50.184
but it hopefully gives you a little

04:50.184 --> 04:52.589
bit of intuition why you can do this.

04:52.905 --> 04:55.414
If you scale up the
function, you're essentially

04:55.414 --> 04:57.900
scaling up the vertical
dimension, so the area

04:57.900 --> 05:00.939
under this is going to just be a scaled up

05:00.939 --> 05:05.054
version of the area under
the original function f of x.

05:05.274 --> 05:07.560
And once again really,
really, really useful

05:07.560 --> 05:12.281
property of definite
integrals that's going to

05:12.281 --> 05:14.497
help us solve a bunch
of definite integrals.

05:14.631 --> 05:18.933
And kind of clarify what
we're even doing with them.
